 <FONT face="verdana,arial,helvetica" size="1" ><B></b></font>
<p><FONT face="verdana, tahoma" size="2" >Ok, here's my rant:<br>
<br>
Numbers in RPGs.  Good or bad?<br>
<br>
This is a thread that I'm fighting a solo battle in on the Horizons boards (so if any of you feel like I do, come help me out.  Otherwise, STFU! hehe).<br>
<br>
Anyway, my position is that the fewer numbers you see in a computer RPG, the better.  The numbers are there to determine the specifics of how the character interacts with the world.  They're not necessary information for the player to have.  Let the computer handle the numbers, and let the player handle the playing.<br>
<br>
My contention is that the deffinition of role playing is that if you are truly roleplaying, you cannot use any information that you, as a player, have but that your character does not (IC vs. OOC).  When you start letting your character act based on OOC information (like how much damage a sword can do vs. a different sword), it's less RP, and more G.  The point of a roleplaying game should be to roleplay, and the more you can separate your IC knowledge from your OOC knowledge the better.<br>
<br>
I don't know if I'm explaining it as well as I can, but my head fills like it's filled with lead right now.  I hate being stuffed up.  Bah!<br>
<br>
So, what do you think?  Do you think that numbers are vital information for the player to have, or should things be done more visually (descriptions, graphics, audible cues)?  Should your sword be "A long sword with a well-balanced steel blade that has been finely honed" or "a +3 long sword"?<br>
<br>
My take is that the first on is real roleplaying, and the second one is just a hack 'n slash l00tfest.  <br>
__________________<br>
If I were creating a world, I wouldn't mess about with butterflies and daffodils! I would've started with lasers, eight o'clock, day one!</font></p>

