 <FONT face="verdana,arial,helvetica" size="1" ><B></b></font>
<p><FONT face="verdana, tahoma" size="2" >Judging from the EQ experience, I'd have to say that it's a nice idea, but not likely to work. Just as the concept of getting actually roleplaying in an MMORPG is unlikely, the idea that people *won't* min/max is also unrealistic. Even if you hide the numbers from the players, they will start to figure it out one way or another. And once they do, then the designers have to figure out how to make challenging encounters with maxxed characters. Which means, if you *don't* have a maxxed character, then the encounters will be much harder, if not impossible.<br>
<br>
Once people figure out that the Giant Blade of Flaying is better than the Well-Honed Longsword, then anyone with a Well-Honed Longsword is at a disadvantage. The min/maxxers (which comprise the vast majority of gamers) will then reject anyone who doesn't have a Giant Blade of Flaying since they will be grouped with a subpar character.<br>
<br>
Part of it, I'm sure, is that a lot of gamers are the computer programmer/engineer types who *want* to know the numbers. They *want* to know the formulas and the strengths and weaknesses of the systems set up in the game. These are the folks who get a new electronic device and immediately take it apart to try and see how it works. Hiding the numbers just translates to either a feeling of being deceived (for the open source types) or a challenge to figure it out despite the designers (for the hacker types). And once the formulas are broken, the information *will* get around (thanks to the 'net).<br>
<br>
Of course, these are the types that also make abyssmal roleplayers. <img src="images/smilies/smile.gif" border=0><br>
<br>
Rick<br>
__________________<br>
I'll try being nicer if you'll try being smarter.</font></p>

