<p><FONT face="verdana, tahoma" size="2" >/em blinks, regarding Shadis oddly<br>
<br>
Woah...didnt expect you to be on THAT side of the arguement. I was sure I had you pegged before I had even read your opinons on the matter. Some points to add to the two wise men before me...<br>
<br>
Dungeon Hack was a very good selling game, and it took the AD&D paper engine and made a single person 'role playing' game out of it. It was fun, easy, and a great waste of time. It was a true powergamers paradise, you could save, reload, look at stats, change them, identify items, and on and on and on. Why did it? Cause it was FUN.<br>
<br>
So the problem is two fold. As mentioned, the players will figure out what weapon hits harder and and what stat does what. Attack the same enemy, with two weapons, which kills it quicker? Do it over and over, and you find the better weapon. To solve this, you would have to have a very large and random factor. And thats gay. People like some rules and control, whether they admit it or not.<br>
<br>
The second part of this issue is not really what the players would do or how sneaky they are, but rather, why? Because they were raised on it. Show me a tabletop RP system that doesnt have numbers. Show me one where the players dont EVENTUALLY learn what the DMs know, or arent encouraged to try DMing themselves. Show me one without tables or charts.<br>
<br>
Part of the beauty of these games is the creation process for Min's sake! Havent we all talked about this before? That making a character is as fun as playing it, and sometimes we never even played one. But to make one and tweak and play and understand the game, you need some knowledge of the skills and stats and whatnot.<br>
<br>
Hmmm. I think there is an in between. Dont tell the players anything more than they need to know. But, be a player yourself and not a dickheaded fuck face GM...that is...tell them what they need to have fun. Dont hide, but dont give away either.<br>
<br>
Balance. An in between of the two extremes.<br>
<br>
/em breathes deeply, calming<br>
<br>
Alchemy works.<br>
<br>
Kith</font></p>

