<p><FONT face="verdana, tahoma" size="2" >DP said:<blockquote><FONT face="verdana,arial,helvetica" size="1" >quote:</font><hr>Of course, these are the types that also make abyssmal roleplayers.<hr></blockquote><br>
That's blatantly untrue. I'm a pretty good roleplayer (and that's not just my own opinion), but I want my numbers. The powergamer types aren't the "GIVE ME NUMBERS" types, they're the "numbers rule everything" types. And, as EQ has shown, if people aren't given the numbers, then by god they're going to <i>take</i> them. Endless log parsings, combat tests, the works. (As a result, of course, people know they were lied to about what stats do and how effective they are, and it also leads to misconceptions like agility affects your attack rate, which it does not.)<br>
<br>
Wanting numbers may or may not make someone a powergamer, but it sure as hell doesn't make them a crappy roleplayer. Hell, I can be an insane raging powergaming monster (I made a weapon in D&D that can decapitate someone on a natural five, and a guy in Shadowrun who rolls can roll eighteen dice a melee attack), but I know to keep it in check.<br>
<br>
Besides, in PnP games, there's a live GM who won't throw you up against things you can't handle. They teach you to roleplay and not stress about the numbers. In console/computer RPGs, there's only the cold unfeeling AI who doesn't make compromises. They teach you that anything less than the utmost efficiency will result in death.<br>
<br>
Guess which teacher most MMORPG folks had?<br>
__________________<br>
Pay attention to me, dammit!<br>
