<p><FONT face="verdana, tahoma" size="2" >I think maybe DP's onto something there as to why some people just have to have numbers and some people don't like them.  It's the whole creative/analytical thing. <br>
<br>
Personally, I'm very much both.  I actually really like to have numbers for things (see, Kith, you were right about me <img src="images/smilies/smile.gif" border=0> ).  But that's because I find it interesting to do the whole logical statistical comparison thing.  I like trying to find out if a sword that does more damage is better, or one that lets me do more attacks per round.  If it's better to dual wield with a penalty to both, or wield one big sword.<br>
<br>
However, when I roleplay, I don't like to have those things get in the way as much.  I like the story.  I like the plot.  I like getting into the adventure.  And when I posess knowledge that my character shouldn't have, it makes it too easy for me to act out of character.  Like if two swords look identical and one is +2 but the other is +3...he shouldn't discard one right away without at least trying them both extensively.  But that's what happens because I'm driving my character and I've got more info than he does.  <br>
<br>
You guys are right that it's more convenient, that people like numbers, that all RPGs have them, and that people will eventually either find them out or come close enough.  And I'm sure most RPGs for the next century will show their numbers to the players.  And I guess I was being a bit harsh when I said that having numbers means you can't roleplay.  I guess it'd be more accurate for me to say that you don't NEED numbers for roleplaying, and that it CAN hinder roleplaying to have too many numbers.  Anyway, I'll always prefer a TRUE roleplaying game (where the emphasis is on roleplaying) to hide the numbers as much as possible.  And I'll always consider any game with lots of numbers to be more of a "fantasy action game" than a roleplaying game.<br>
<br>
At least you guys are more intelligent than the guys on the Horizons board who said that you HAVE to have numbers.  Just because you WANT to see the numbers doesn't mean you HAVE to.<br>
<br>
Oh, and if anyone ever wants to try an RPG without seeing the numbers, try playing a MUSH.  Most of them are player-driven and not numbers-driven.  They can be lots of fun.<br>
__________________<br>
If I were creating a world, I wouldn't mess about with butterflies and daffodils! I would've started with lasers, eight o'clock, day one!</font></p>

